# Q.5 a) — When static vs dynamic binding are useful (with short example)

**Static binding (compile-time)**
- Used for regular (non-virtual) functions.
- Faster, resolved at compile time.
- Useful when behavior should not change by derived classes (e.g., utility/helper functions).

**Dynamic binding (runtime)**
- Achieved via `virtual` functions in C++.
- Useful when you want polymorphic behavior — call the derived class’s override through a base pointer/reference.
- Example: GUI base class `Widget` with virtual `draw()` — each derived widget draws differently.

**Short code example (dynamic binding):**
```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Base {
public:
    virtual void show() { cout << "Base show\n"; } // virtual -> dynamic binding
};

class Derived : public Base {
public:
    void show() override { cout << "Derived show\n"; }
};

int main() {
    Base *b = new Derived();
    b->show(); // prints "Derived show" because of dynamic binding
    delete b;
}
```

